Sigh.

> Someone correct me if I am wrong, but when it
> comes to actually using two-weapon combat, this is
> more or less the way of it. The penalty/ bonus
> used in the "to hit" and damage calculations
> always comes from the two-weapon combat table:

> Rank       +HIT/+DAM
> ---------- ---------
> Unskilled:   -9/-3
> Basic:       -7/-1
> Skilled:     -5/0
> Expert:      -3/+1

> Your skill for this table is considered to be the
> lesser of your skill in your primary weapon and
> your skill in two-weapon combat. Assume you are
> two-weaponing with a long sword in primary and a
> mace in secondary. If your skill in long sword is
> skilled, and your skill in two-weapon is
> unskilled, you will get -9 to hit and -3 to
> damage. The same would be true if you were
> unskilled in long sword and skilled in two-weapon.
> If you are skilled in long sword *and* two-weapon,
> you would get -5 to hit and +0 to damage. Your
> skill in mace would not matter at all. To that
> end, you can wield whatever you'd like for your
> secondary weapon. If your primary hits, your
> secondary will also hit except for a few weird
> scenarios. If you plan to use a particular weapon
> type as your primary weapon only when
> two-weaponing, you need not advance its skill
> beyond your skill in two-weapon combat.

>> 2) Does the correct answer, whichever one it is,
>> make any sense to anyone?

> Probably. I've never really thought that much
> about it. Offhand, it seems like it should
> exercise two-weapon and your primary weapon skill
> at half the normal rate. The secondary weapon
> skill doesn't factor into the process, so it
> shouldn't be effected.

> I suppose the argument is that your secondary
> weapon is getting a "free ride" unless it requires
> more work to effectively use two-weapon combat.
> That is, consider what happens if you two-weapon
> with a longsword in primary, with expert in
> longsword and expert in two- weapon. Regardless of
> what weapon you put in your second hand, you hit
> with it as if you had expert skill, since the
> secondary always hits if your primary hits (except
> for those few exotic situations). Granted, you're
> only getting -3/+1 instead of the +3/+2 that you
> get for Expert skill in single-weapon combat, but
> you have to weight that against the fact that you
> do a lot more damage in the first place. The
> awkward skill system is probably just an attempt
> to offset that advantage.

